DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2026.106793
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1386505626005332
KW  - Perioperative data integration
KW  - Extract–Transform–Load
KW  - Cardiac surgery
KW  - Data quality
KW  - Learning health system
KW  - eHealth
AB  - Background
Cardiac surgery under cardiopulmonary bypass generates large volumes of perioperative data fragmented across multiple clinical information systems, limiting their integration for quality improvement and research. Cardiac surgery registries still rely predominantly on manual data entry, which is time-consuming, error-prone, and often incomplete.
Objective
To report the real-world implementation of an automated perioperative data integration platform and evaluate its performance in terms of data completeness, accuracy, clinical workflow, and patient safety.
Methods
We developed CPB-REPORT, a secure web-based platform using an Extract–Transform–Load architecture, integrating data from electronic health records, anaesthesia, and critical care systems into a unified database aligned with the national cardiac surgery registry. The platform was deployed in a high-volume cardiac surgery centre and initially combined automated extraction of registry variables and structured manual entry. A concordance study was performed to evaluate the robustness of automatically integrated data.
Results
Between May 2022 and May 2026, CPB-REPORT prospectively captured all cardiac surgery procedures performed at the institution: 2,915 patients underwent 2,927 procedures, each documented across 174 fields and transmitted to the national registry. Compared with the previous fully manual process, the platform reduced data entry time by 20 min per patient. Dataset completeness improved from 68 % at platform deployment to 90 % in 2025. The concordance study showed an overall concordance of 97.8 % between automatically extracted and source-record values (95 % CI: 94.7–99.4 %), with excellent intraclass correlation coefficients for continuous variables. No cybersecurity incident was occurred. Real-time outcome monitoring enabled early detection of a surgical site infection cluster in 2022.
Conclusion
The present work demonstrates that Extract–Transform–Load-based perioperative data integration is achievable in routine cardiac surgery practice using standard hospital infrastructure. The platform offers a transferable methodological model for integrating clinical information systems with international registries in line with European data-protection and interoperability frameworks.
ER  - 

