AB  - Additive manufacturing research increasingly relies on digital process chains that generate heterogeneous data from sources such as geometry, process parameters, sensors, materials, and quality evaluations. These data are often fragmented across tools, systems, and institutions, limiting their interoperability and reuse. Within the concept of the data-driven digital twin, the digital shadow serves as the operational layer for structured data aggregation and is fundamental for the data exchange between the physical and digital entity. Their potential for integrating data across different institutions is especially relevant in research and innovation-driven environments in additive manufacturing. This study, in form of a systematic literature review (SLR), aims to investigate how current research addresses the harmonization of heterogeneous and fragmented data through digital shadows, and to identify relevant research areas that contribute to designing interoperable data spaces in science and engineering. This review is conducted across four central topics: data fragmentation, interoperability, digital shadows, and data spaces, with six clusters derived from pairwise intersections. For each cluster, the retrieved publications were analyzed to identify conceptual frameworks, architectural patterns, integration mechanisms, and use-case references relevant to interoperable data exchange. The defined clusters focus on specific technical aspects of interoperability, including semantic data modeling, federated exchange mechanisms, and governance structures, with a focus on system-level implementation. In contrast, methodologically grounded and integrative approaches that synthesize these aspects into cohesive engineering frameworks remain absent. Although additive manufacturing is referenced as a representative domain, existing literature inadequately addresses its specific requirements with regard to data heterogeneity, traceability, and structured reuse of research data across institutional boundaries. This paper consolidates the fragmented literature into a structured overview and presents a conceptual model that brings together the identified clusters. The model highlights key challenges and open questions that need to be addressed when developing a domain-oriented data space for additive manufacturing research.
ER  - 

